            <ArticleTitle>Coronary-pulmonary arteriovenous fistula used as proximal anastomotic site for saphenous vein grafts in patient with porcelain aorta.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary artery bypass grafting in patients with porcelain aorta and calcified great vessels is associated with a high risk of systemic embolism. Various techniques have been suggested to minimize that risk. We describe the case of a patient with left main coronary disease and a severely calcified ascending aorta, who could not undergo cardiopulmonary bypass. To the best of our knowledge, this is the 1st reported use of a congenital coronary-pulmonary arteriovenous fistula as a proximal anastomotic site for saphenous vein grafts, to achieve optimal revascularization in a patient with porcelain aorta.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
